            <ArticleTitle>Glasgow prognostic score is a prognosis predictor for patients with endometrial cancer.</ArticleTitle>
            <Pagination>
                <MedlinePgn>355-359</MedlinePgn>
            </Pagination>
            <ELocationID EIdType="pii" ValidYN="Y">S0301-2115(17)30024-6</ELocationID>
            <ELocationID EIdType="doi" ValidYN="Y">10.1016/j.ejogrb.2017.01.024</ELocationID>
            <Abstract>
                <AbstractText Label="OBJECTIVE" NlmCategory="OBJECTIVE">This study investigated whether the inflammation-based Glasgow prognostic score (GPS) predicted the prognosis of patients with endometrial cancer (EC) in terms of progression-free survival (PFS) and overall survival (OS).</AbstractText>
                <AbstractText Label="STUDY DESIGN" NlmCategory="METHODS">Pretreatment GPS was examined to determine the correlations with recurrence and survival in 431 patients with EC. Statistical analyses were performed using the Mann-Whitney U test. PFS and OS were analyzed using the Kaplan-Meier method. Cox's proportional hazard regression was used for univariate and multivariate analyses.</AbstractText>
                <AbstractText Label="RESULTS" NlmCategory="RESULTS">Median PFS and OS were 49.7 and 52.7 months, respectively. The follow-up range was 1 to 140 months. Kaplan-Meier analysis revealed that patients with EC cancer and high GPS (GPS 2) had a shorter PFS and OS than those with lower GPS (GPS 0+1) (PFS: P&lt;0.001; OS; P&lt;0.001). On multivariate analysis, GPS (GPS 2) was an independent predictor of both recurrence (P&lt;0.001) and survival (P&lt;0.001) for all cases of EC.</AbstractText>
                <AbstractText Label="CONCLUSION" NlmCategory="CONCLUSIONS">GPS can be useful as an indicator of poor prognosis in patients with EC.</AbstractText>
